(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["cam-nutrition-exam-2-introduction-to-cam-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Introduction to CAM (Set 2) — CAM/Nutrition Exam 2","category":"CAM / Nutrition","questions":[{"q":"What is the stated long-term goal of biofeedback training?","choices":["To identify which medications the patient can safely discontinue","To teach patients to self-regulate without reliance on monitoring equipment","To produce a permanent change in resting brain anatomy","To establish the patient's dominant dosha before treatment planning"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that through repeated practice and feedback patients learn to modify physiologic functions, with the goal of self-regulation without relying on the monitoring equipment. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"Which question does the lecture recommend clinicians raise when a patient is considering a complementary therapy?","choices":["Does the patient's family approve of the proposed therapy?","Does the therapy require patients to stop conventional medical treatments?","Was the therapy first described in writing before the beginning of the twentieth century?","Is the therapy available in more than one country worldwide?"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. This is one of the lecture's explicit counseling questions, along with asking about research support, substantial side effects, unfounded claims, travel, licensure, institutional backing, and expense. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What is naturopathy founded on, and where does it direct its attention?","choices":["The measurement of physiologic signals, directing attention toward learned self-regulation","The insertion of needles at defined points, directing attention toward the flow of the vital force","The healing power of nature, directing attention toward the cause of disease rather than the symptoms alone","The balance of opposing forces, directing attention toward harmony with the external environment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes naturopathy as founded on the healing power of nature, emphasizing prevention, a healthy lifestyle, treatment of the whole person, and finding the cause of disease rather than just treating symptoms. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What safety concern does the lecture raise specifically about Chinese herbal products?","choices":["They are effective only when combined with acupuncture in the same session","Some have been found to contain contaminants including heavy metals, pesticides, and microorganisms","They lose all activity if taken with any food or beverage","They are uniformly supported by consistent high-quality clinical evidence across every condition studied"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture reports that some Chinese herbal products have been found to contain heavy metals, pesticides, and microorganisms, and adds that herb-drug interactions should be considered when counseling patients. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about the use of dietary supplements?","choices":["They require a prescription in most states before they may be dispensed","They are among the most commonly used complementary health approaches, and patients often use them without telling clinicians","They are only rarely used, because most patients strongly prefer therapies delivered directly by a practitioner","They carry no meaningful risk of interaction with prescription medications"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes supplements as among the most commonly used complementary approaches, widely available without prescription, and often used without being discussed with healthcare professionals. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"On what does chiropractic medicine primarily focus, according to the lecture?","choices":["Diagnosis and management of musculoskeletal conditions, particularly those involving the spine","Diagnosis and management of nutritional deficiency, particularly of vitamins and minerals","Diagnosis and management of stress-related disorders, particularly anxiety and insomnia","Diagnosis and management of heavy metal toxicity, particularly lead and mercury"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes chiropractic medicine as focused on musculoskeletal conditions especially of the spine, using spinal manipulation and other manual therapy to improve mobility and reduce pain. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which five elements form the basis of many traditional Chinese medicine concepts, per the lecture?","choices":["Space, air, fire, water, and earth","Blood, phlegm, bile, lymph, and marrow","Wind, heat, cold, damp, and dryness","Earth, fire, water, wood, and metal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists earth, fire, water, wood, and metal as the five elements believed to represent patterns and relationships within nature and the human body. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"The lecture adds an important caveat to the five-domain model. What is it?","choices":["The model is used only outside the United States and has no application domestically","Each individual therapy belongs to exactly one of the five domains, which is what makes classifying any new practice straightforward in clinical teaching","The model has been formally replaced by a system based on route of administration","Many therapies overlap categories, and current efforts emphasize evaluating evidence, safety, and effectiveness of specific practices rather than classifying them"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes the five-domain model is commonly used for teaching but that many therapies overlap, and that current efforts emphasize evaluating the evidence for specific practices. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which activity does the lecture describe as a core function of the National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health?","choices":["Conducting and supporting research on the safety, effectiveness, and mechanisms of complementary health practices","Issuing and renewing the practice licenses of complementary and alternative medicine practitioners in each of the fifty states","Certifying which dietary supplements may be legally sold without a prescription","Accrediting the four-year graduate programs that train naturopathic physicians"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists exactly this function, along with providing evidence-based information for patients and healthcare professionals. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 10"},{"q":"A patient stops his prescribed antihypertensive entirely and treats his blood pressure only with herbal preparations. This is best classified as which of the following?","choices":["Complementary medicine, since herbal products supplement the conventional regimen","Alternative medicine, since the therapy is used in place of conventional treatment","Integrative medicine, since more than one treatment modality is now involved","Functional medicine, since an underlying contributor is being addressed"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines alternative medicine as an approach used in place of conventional medicine, which is precisely what replacing the antihypertensive represents. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How is cupping performed and what is it thought to do?","choices":["A vacuum is created inside a cup placed on skin, and the resulting suction elevates skin and underlying tissue","Repeated strokes are applied with a smooth-edged instrument along the skin","Pressure is applied to specific areas of the feet, hands, or ears","Thin needles are inserted at defined points on the body in order to influence the flow of the vital force"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes creating a vacuum inside a cup placed on the skin, with suction elevating skin and underlying tissue, and notes cupping is thought to increase local blood flow. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"In Ayurvedic theory as presented in the lecture, which dosha is associated with digestion, metabolism, and temperature?","choices":["Pitta, formed from fire and water","Vata, formed from space and air","Kapha, formed from water and earth","Qi, formed from yin and yang"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture pairs Pitta with fire and water and associates it with digestion, metabolism, and temperature. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does the lecture define the focus of mind-body medicine?","choices":["The use of naturally occurring substances to promote health or manage disease","The application of hands-on technique to the musculoskeletal system and soft tissues","The interactions between the brain, mind, body, and behavior and their influence on health","The manipulation of proposed energy fields believed to surround and permeate the body"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines mind-body medicine as focusing on interactions among the brain, mind, body, and behavior and how those interactions influence health. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Which environmental exposure source does the lecture pair correctly with its metal?","choices":["Well water and rice are common sources of environmental lead exposure","Old paint and plumbing are common sources of environmental arsenic exposure","Fish is a common source of environmental mercury exposure","Leafy vegetables are a common source of environmental cadmium exposure"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists lead in old paint and plumbing, arsenic in well water and rice, and mercury in fish as common sources of environmental metal exposure. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"Which therapies does the lecture name as examples that aim to manipulate proposed energy fields?","choices":["Homeopathy, naturopathy, and Ayurvedic dosha balancing techniques","Chiropractic, osteopathic manipulation, and therapeutic massage technique","Chelation, botanical medicine, and specialized therapeutic dietary patterns","Reiki, therapeutic touch, and acupuncture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names Reiki, therapeutic touch, and acupuncture as therapies that aim to manipulate proposed energy fields to promote healing. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"From what sources may homeopathic products be derived, per the lecture?","choices":["Only from flowering plants that have documented pharmacologic activity","Plants such as red onion and belladonna, minerals such as arsenic, and animals such as crushed whole bees","Only from minerals and metals that have been purified to remove organic material","Only from cultured microorganisms grown under controlled laboratory conditions"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture gives exactly these examples of plant, mineral, and animal sources for homeopathic products. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"How does the lecture distinguish visualization from guided imagery?","choices":["Visualization always requires supervision by a trained therapist, whereas guided imagery is by definition entirely self-directed by the patient","Visualization is used only for pain, whereas guided imagery is used only for anxiety","Visualization refers to seeing images in the mind's eye, whereas guided imagery uses one or more senses to create a detailed mental experience","Visualization occurs during sleep, whereas guided imagery occurs while fully awake"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture defines visualization as \"seeing\" images in the mind's eye and guided imagery as engaging one or more senses to build a detailed mental experience. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"On what belief are energy therapies based, and what does the lecture say about that basis?","choices":["That the measurable electrical currents generated within skeletal muscle can be deliberately retrained, a mechanism that is now well established experimentally","That plant compounds act on receptor systems, which has been confirmed pharmacologically","That biofields influence health and healing, though their existence and clinical significance have not been conclusively demonstrated scientifically","That structural misalignment causes disease, which imaging studies have verified"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states energy therapies rest on the belief that biofields influence health and healing, and adds that the existence and clinical significance of subtle biofields have not been conclusively demonstrated scientifically. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which description best matches the lecture's account of functional medicine?","choices":["A whole-person approach focused on potential underlying contributors to disease, considering lifestyle, nutrition, environment, genetics, and psychosocial factors","A system of care organized around balancing three fundamental body energies determined at birth","A therapy that uses electronic monitoring so patients can learn to control physiologic responses","An approach built entirely on the principle that a substance capable of producing symptoms in a healthy person can be used to treat those same symptoms in someone who is ill"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes functional medicine as whole-person and patient-centered, focused on underlying contributors to disease and weighing lifestyle, nutrition, environment, genetics, and psychosocial factors. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"The lecture contrasts functional medicine with naturopathic medicine. Which statement captures that distinction as presented?","choices":["Functional medicine is a way clinicians practice, whereas naturopathic medicine is a profession with its own philosophy and training","Functional medicine is a licensed profession, whereas naturopathic medicine is only a philosophy of care","Functional medicine may only be practiced by naturopathic doctors after additional certification","Functional medicine deliberately avoids all nutrition and lifestyle counseling, whereas naturopathic medicine relies on those strategies almost exclusively"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states this contrast directly: functional medicine is an approach to patient care usable by physicians, physician assistants, and nurse practitioners, while naturopathic medicine is a distinct healthcare profession practiced by naturopathic doctors. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"The lecture raises a specific safety concern about Ayurvedic preparations. What is it?","choices":["They are frequently contaminated with prescription antibiotics during manufacturing","Some include metals, minerals, or gems, and testing has found high levels of lead and mercury","They must be refrigerated continuously or they lose all therapeutic activity","They are dangerous only when combined with traditional Chinese herbal products"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lecture notes that some Ayurvedic preparations include metals, minerals, or gems, that one in four supplements tested had high lead levels and almost half had high mercury levels, and that the Food and Drug Administration warns some products may be potentially harmful. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"According to the lecture, which feature most clearly distinguishes integrative medicine from simply using a complementary therapy?","choices":["It requires the patient to discontinue all prescription medications first","It restricts care exclusively to those therapies that have already been formally reviewed and approved by the Food and Drug Administration","It is limited to practices that originated outside of Western medicine","It is a patient-centered, whole-person approach that deliberately combines conventional care with evidence-based complementary therapies"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes integrative medicine as patient-centered and whole-person, combining conventional medicine with evidence-based complementary therapies while emphasizing wellness, prevention, and the therapeutic relationship. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A patient asks about ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid infusions marketed for heart disease. What does the lecture conclude?","choices":["Evidence for cardiovascular benefit is limited and controversial, and it should not substitute for evidence-based treatment","Evidence supports routine use as first-line therapy for coronary artery disease","The therapy carries essentially no risk at all, because the agent involved is a naturally occurring compound","The therapy has no established medical use in any clinical situation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that evidence for cardiovascular benefit is limited and remains controversial, that chelation is not recommended as a substitute for evidence-based cardiovascular treatment, and that risks include kidney injury, hypocalcemia, and infusion-related complications. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"How does the lecture describe the mechanism by which meditation may help?","choices":["By binding circulating metals so that they are more readily excreted","By elevating skin temperature enough to relax the underlying musculature","By realigning structural relationships within the spine and adjacent joints","By promoting a physiologic relaxation response and increasing awareness of thoughts, emotions, and behaviors"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states that meditation promotes a physiologic relaxation response, increases awareness of thoughts, emotions, and behaviors, and helps reduce automatic reactions to stress. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A patient with tension-type headache is referred for a technique in which skin sensors detect electrical activity related to muscle tension and display it back to the patient. Which method is this?","choices":["Breathing biofeedback therapy, using sensors on the chest and abdomen","Guided imagery, using one or more senses to build a mental experience","Electromyographic biofeedback, with sensors attached to the skin","Progressive muscle relaxation, tensing and releasing muscle groups in sequence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture describes electromyographic biofeedback as using skin sensors to detect electrical activity related to muscle tension, with indications including tension-type headache, migraine, physical rehabilitation, and pelvic floor dysfunction. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Which pair of reasons for the popularity of these approaches is drawn directly from the lecture?","choices":["Guaranteed product purity and mandatory practitioner licensure in all states","Lower cost in every case and universal insurance coverage for these services","A perception of being natural and safe, and dissatisfaction with conventional outcomes","Faster onset of effect and stronger regulatory oversight than prescription drugs"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The lecture lists both, along with wanting more control over one's health, interest in holistic care, easy access to products and services, and the value placed on practitioners' time and attention. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What does the lecture say about the evidence behind specialized diet therapies?","choices":["Every dietary pattern has been shown equally effective across all chronic conditions","Dietary patterns should be recommended without regard to individual patient factors","Diet therapy has no role in preventing or managing chronic disease of any kind","Evidence varies by dietary pattern and condition, and nutritional adequacy must be considered"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture names Mediterranean, plant-based, and intermittent fasting patterns, notes that evidence varies, and directs that nutritional adequacy and patient-specific factors be weighed. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"The lecture notes a labeling caution about homeopathic products. What is it?","choices":["Products labeled homeopathic must always list the original substance concentration","Products labeled homeopathic are exempt from listing any active ingredients","Products labeled as homeopathic are always diluted well beyond the point at which any active molecules could remain","Some products labeled homeopathic may not be highly diluted and can contain substantial amounts of active ingredients"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The lecture warns that some products labeled as homeopathic may not be highly diluted and can contain substantial amounts of active ingredients, which matters for patient safety counseling. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 24"},{"q":"Which principle does the lecture give for biofeedback?","choices":["\"Anything that can be measured can be changed\"","\"Let food be thy medicine\"","\"Like cures like\" in matched symptom patterns","\"Health is balance between opposing forces\""],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The lecture states this as the biofeedback principle, supported by electronic monitoring that lets patients gain control over physiologic responses through training and practice. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Which of the following is one of the five domains of complementary and alternative medicine presented in the lecture?","choices":["Manipulative and body-based practices","Pharmacologic and surgical interventions","Diagnostic imaging and laboratory testing","Emergency and critical care services"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The five domains are whole medical systems, mind-body medicine, biologically based practices, manipulative and body-based practices, and energy therapies. — Introduction to CAM 2026.pptx, Slide 16"}]};
